is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: router1’s access interface eth1 (the subnet for pc1, 195.11.14.0/24) is down at Layer 2.

Mechanism: With router1 eth1 down, ARP from pc1 to its default gateway 195.11.14.1 never receives replies. pc1’s neighbor table shows “195.11.14.1 FAILED,” so any traffic to the gateway (and thus to pc2 via routing) fails. pc1’s NIC can still report link up locally, but the peer side on router1 is down, so no frames are exchanged (pc1 shows 0 RX packets).

Localization: On router1, ip link and ethtool confirm eth1 is DOWN with no link detected:
- router1 ip addr/ip link: “eth1 … state DOWN,” flags lack UP/LOWER_UP.
- router1 ethtool eth1: “Link detected: no.”
- router1 ifconfig eth1 shows no RX/TX and DOWN state despite IP 195.11.14.1/24 configured.
On pc1: eth0 is UP with 195.11.14.2/24 and default via 195.11.14.1, but ARP to 195.11.14.1 fails. Meanwhile, the core inter-router link is fine (router1 eth0 and router2 links up; pc2 reaches 200.1.1.1), so the failure is isolated to router1’s eth1.

Root cause: router1’s customer-facing interface eth1 (195.11.14.1/24) was administratively or physically taken down, breaking the L2 link to pc1.

Corrective fix: On router1, bring the interface up and verify carrier: “ip link set dev eth1 up”; confirm “ethtool eth1” shows “Link detected: yes.” Recheck ARP/ping from pc1 to 195.11.14.1, then end-to-end to 200.1.1.2. No FRR/BGP changes are required.
=== END ===